#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:1
msgid "Writing a unit test"
msgstr "编写单元测试"

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:3
msgid ""
"Unit tests call the C++ functions directly and check what they return. They "
"are the cheapest tests to write and the fastest to run — the whole suite "
"finishes in about a second — so they are the right place for anything that "
"can be checked without running a whole program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:8
msgid ""
"This page is the walkthrough for adding one. See "
"[Testing](testing.md#testing) for what the three test layers are and how to "
"run them all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:12
msgid "What can be tested this way"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:14
msgid ""
"The unit tests link `cneta_core`, the static library built from the shared "
"sources (`model.cpp`, `tree_op.cpp`, `parse_cn.cpp`, `stats.cpp`, "
"`likelihood.cpp` and the rest). Any function declared in one of their "
"headers can be called from a test."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:19
msgid ""
"Functions defined in `cnets.cpp`, `cnetml.cpp` or `cnetmcmc.cpp` cannot: "
"those files are compiled into the three programs, not into the library, so a"
" test that calls them will fail to link. Behaviour that only exists inside "
"one of the programs belongs in `tests/e2e/` instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:24
msgid "Adding a test file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:26
msgid "There are two steps, and the second is easy to forget."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:28
msgid ""
"**Create `tests/unit/test_<area>.cpp`** — or, if one of the existing files "
"already covers the area, add your `TEST_CASE` there instead. The four files "
"are `test_model.cpp`, `test_tree_op.cpp`, `test_parse_cn.cpp` and "
"`test_stats.cpp`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:33
msgid ""
"**List the new file** in the `add_executable` block of "
"`tests/unit/CMakeLists.txt`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:46
msgid ""
"A file that is not listed there is never compiled. There is no error message"
" — your tests simply do not exist. If a test you just wrote does not appear "
"in the output, check this first."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:51
msgid "Building and running"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:53
msgid "From the repository root:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:61
msgid ""
"The first command is only needed once, and again whenever you edit "
"`CMakeLists.txt`. After that, rebuild and re-run with the last two commands."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:64
msgid ""
"Note that configuring alone builds nothing — if you skip the `cmake --build`"
" step, `ctest` runs the previous executable and your new test will not be "
"there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:67
msgid "To run only your own tests while working on them:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:80
msgid ""
"Running the executable directly also shows anything the code prints to "
"`stdout`, which `ctest` hides unless a test fails."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:83
msgid "The shape of a test"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:94
msgid ""
"The **name** is what `ctest` prints, so write it as a statement of what must"
" be true rather than as a label. \"returns the largest node time\" tells a "
"future reader what broke; \"test1\" does not."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:97
msgid ""
"The **tag** in square brackets groups tests so they can be run together. Use"
" the one already used by the file you are working in: `[model]`, "
"`[tree_op]`, `[parse_cn]` or `[stats]`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:100
msgid ""
"**`REQUIRE`** stops the test case at the first failure. Use `CHECK` instead "
"if you want the remaining assertions in that test to run anyway."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:103
msgid "Comparing numbers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:105
msgid ""
"`REQUIRE(a == b)` is right for integers, states and sizes. For `double` "
"values it is almost always wrong: arithmetic that is mathematically "
"equivalent can differ in the last bits, so an exact comparison fails for no "
"useful reason."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:109
msgid ""
"Compare doubles with a tolerance instead, using one of the two matchers from"
" `<catch2/matchers/catch_matchers_floating_point.hpp>`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "Matcher"
msgstr ""

