msgid "Use it when"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "Example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "`WithinAbs(expected, tol)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "the expected value is zero or close to it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "a rate-matrix row must sum to `0.0`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "`WithinRel(expected, tol)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "everything else — the tolerance scales with the size of the number"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:45
msgid "a likelihood, a branch length, a rate"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:122
msgid ""
"`WithinRel` cannot be used against an expected value of zero: a relative "
"tolerance around zero is zero, so the comparison becomes exact again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:125
msgid "Matrices are stored column-major"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:127
msgid ""
"Every rate and transition matrix in the codebase is a flat `double*` in "
"column-major order, so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:134
msgid ""
"Writing `m[j + i * n]` by mistake reads the transpose. That is worth being "
"careful about, because many of these matrices are close to symmetric, so a "
"transposed test can still pass and hide the error. `test_model.cpp` has a "
"small `row_sum` helper that shows the indexing in use."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:139
msgid "Checking the same property for several inputs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:141
msgid ""
"When a property should hold for many values, loop over them and use "
"`CAPTURE` so that a failure reports which values caused it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:154
msgid ""
"Without `CAPTURE`, a failure tells you the assertion broke but not which "
"iteration broke it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:157
msgid ""
"Choose the values deliberately. Inputs that are equal to each other, or "
"zero, can make a wrong result look identical to a right one — if two rates "
"are both `0.01`, then code that uses them in the wrong places still produces"
" the correct matrix and the test cannot detect the mistake. Varying the "
"values, and the ratios between them, is what gives the test its power."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:163
msgid "Using a fixture file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:165
msgid ""
"`tests/data/` holds a small committed dataset. Tests reach it through "
"`CNETA_TEST_DATA_DIR`, a path that `tests/unit/CMakeLists.txt` defines at "
"compile time so that tests work regardless of the directory `ctest` runs "
"them from:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:176
msgid ""
"`test_tree_op.cpp` and `test_parse_cn.cpp` both use this. Prefer an existing"
" fixture over adding a new one; if you do add a file, commit it on its own "
"with an explanation of what generated it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:180
msgid "Recording a bug you cannot fix yet"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:182
msgid ""
"If a test documents a genuine bug that is not going to be fixed in the same "
"change, tag it `[!mayfail]`. It still runs and still reports, but it does "
"not fail the build:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/developer-guide/writing-tests.md:190
msgid ""
"Write the assertion the way it *should* pass, add a row to the known-"
"failures table in `tests/README.md` explaining the underlying problem, and "
"remove the tag when the bug is fixed. This is much better than deleting the "
"test or weakening it until it passes."
msgstr ""

